for _spec in SPECS:
    if _spec['ref']=='17.8':
        _spec['languageWhy']={
          'csharp':'C# sorts an int[] of original indices and compares dimensions with CompareTo, so the supplied person rows never move. The predecessor walk collects those original indices before reversing them; returning sorted-array positions would identify different people.',
          'java':'Java uses an Integer[] index array because Arrays.sort with a custom row comparator cannot directly sort primitive int indices. It reconstructs backward into an int[] of exactly the recorded best length, retaining original person IDs without another reverse-copy step.',
          'cpp':'C++ sorts only a private vector of original indices while people remains a const reference. Predecessors refer to positions in that private order; reconstruction explicitly maps each position back through order before returning the IDs.',
          'python':'Python key sorting caches each height/weight/index tuple once. DP predecessors index this private order, so reconstruction appends order[position], not position itself. Copying rows or sorting people in place would break the caller identity/order contract.',
          'javascript':'JavaScript supplies numeric height/weight/index comparisons rather than default lexical array sorting. All dimension differences are exact in the admitted range. The saved predecessor positions are mapped back to original indices before reversing the local result.'}
    if _spec['ref']=='17.26':
        _spec['languageWhy']={
          'csharp':'C# Dictionary counters use the sparse key i*n+j for document positions i<j. This is a unique address below n*n, not a dense matrix allocation. Output maps through documentIds and uses CompareTo for ordering, avoiding overflow from subtracting arbitrary signed IDs.',
          'java':'Java HashMap stores only positive pair counters, keyed by i*n+j. Posting lists are built in increasing document position order, so each shared-word pair has one orientation. Integer.compare orders original signed IDs safely after decoding the position key.',
          'cpp':'C++ unordered_map stores only observed posting pairs. Position keys stay below 40000, and union counts stay below 401, so the signed arithmetic/casts are safe. The final vector is sorted lexicographically after positions are mapped to the supplied IDs.',
          'python':'Python divmod decodes each sparse i*n+j counter back to document positions. The actual IDs may be negative or in a different order, so the returned pair is normalized separately. Dictionary counters count shared words; no decimal division or fraction reduction changes those counts.',
          'javascript':'JavaScript Map keys encode document positions below 40000, exactly representable as Numbers. Original signed IDs are normalized only when constructing output rows; their numeric sort differences also remain exact. Counts are integer intersection/union sizes, not rounded ratios.'}

SPECS += [dict(ref='17.7',method='TrueNameFrequencies',params=[('names','as'),('frequencies','ai'),('synonyms','aai')],result='aai',mutation='none',
 description='Aggregate baby-name counts across synonymous spellings. Synonym pairs are undirected and transitive. Return one [representativeNameIndex,totalFrequency] row for each equivalence group, including isolated names and zero-frequency groups. The representative may be ANY original name in its group, and result rows may be in any order.',
 constraints=['names has 0..200 distinct nonnull ASCII-letter names, each length 1..20; names are case-sensitive unless linked explicitly. frequencies has the same length with values 0..1000000, so all totals fit signed 32-bit integers. A returned name index refers to this original names array.', 'synonyms contains 0..10000 rows [firstNameIndex,secondNameIndex] with valid indices. Duplicate pairs, reverse duplicates and self-pairs are allowed and do not add frequency. Include an uncounted spelling in names with frequency 0 if it is needed as a synonym-chain bridge.', 'Every original name belongs to exactly one output group, represented once. Sum each name\'s given frequency once, not once per edge. Preserve all arrays, synonym row objects, values and order.'],
 why='Synonymous names are connected components of an undirected graph. Build adjacency from the given pairs, visit each name once, and sum its frequency into that component. Mark names when adding them to the work queue so repeated/cyclic synonym declarations cannot double-count a name.',
 correctness='A graph path is exactly a chain of synonym declarations, so connected components coincide with the required equivalence groups. Starting at an unvisited name, traversal reaches every and only name in its component. Marking on discovery makes each name enter once, so adding its frequency once yields the exact component total despite duplicate/self edges. Later starts skip visited components, producing one row per group. The starting name is an original member and therefore a valid representative; no lexical or insertion-order representative is required.',
 walkthrough='For names=[John,Jon,Johnny,Chris], frequencies=[10,3,0,4] and pairs [[0,1],[1,2]], the first three spellings form one group totaling 13, including the zero-count bridge Johnny. Chris remains a separate group with 4. [[0,13],[3,4]] and [[2,13],[3,4]] are both valid results.',example=([['John','Jon','Johnny','Chris'],[10,3,0,4],[[0,1],[1,2]]],[[0,13],[3,4]]),
 time='O(n+e) traversal time for n names and e listed synonym pairs, using direct index adjacency; duplicate pair contributions are still visited only as listed edges.',space='O(n+e) adjacency, visited/work queue and returned group rows.',edges=['No names, isolated names and all-zero frequencies.','Transitive synonym chains, zero-frequency bridge spellings, duplicate/reverse/self pairs.','Any original representative and arbitrary group-row order are accepted.'],related='Original name indices replace map key strings without losing spelling identity. The author component-frequency task permits an arbitrary representative; this grader preserves that freedom rather than demanding one canonical name.')]
