SPECS += [dict(ref='17.20',method='ContinuousMedians',params=[('values','ai')],result='ad',mutation='none',
 description='Process an integer stream in its given order and return the median immediately after each insertion. The median of an odd-sized prefix is its middle value in sorted order. For an even-sized prefix it is the arithmetic mean of the two middle values, using real arithmetic. Return one numeric value per input element; empty input returns [].',
 constraints=['values is a nonnull array of 0..10000 signed 32-bit integers. Duplicates count as separate stream occurrences. Preserve the input array, order and every value.', 'Return actual medians, not doubled medians or truncated integers. Each admitted median is an integer or half-integer and is exactly representable as a double/Number/float. The result must match these values exactly. Compute even-prefix means without overflowing a signed 32-bit sum.'],
 why='Keep the smaller half in a max heap and the larger half in a min heap. Their roots expose the two values surrounding the middle, so a median query needs no sorting. Balance sizes so the lower heap has either the same count or one extra occurrence; its root alone is then the odd median.',
 correctness='Insert each new value into the lower heap when it is no larger than its maximum, otherwise into the upper heap. This preserves that every lower value is at most every upper value. Moving a root between heaps restores size difference zero or one while preserving that order. For odd prefix length, the lower root has exactly half the other occurrences on either side and is the median. For even length, the lower maximum and upper minimum are the two central occurrences, so their widened average is the required median. Each insertion contributes one occurrence and each output observes the invariant after rebalancing.',
 walkthrough='For [5,1,9,2], the prefixes have sorted forms [5], [1,5], [1,5,9] and [1,2,5,9]. Medians are [5,3,5,3.5]. For [-2147483648,2147483647], the second median is -0.5; widening before summing prevents overflow.',example=([[5,1,9,2]],[5,3,5,3.5]),time='O(n log(n+1)) total insertion time and O(1) median work per prefix.',space='O(n) heap storage plus O(n) returned medians; both heaps retain every inserted occurrence.',edges=['Empty stream, single element and equal repeated values.','Increasing/decreasing streams, alternating extremes and negative half-integer medians.','Full signed endpoints and 10000 insertions.'],related='The public stream API inserts a number and queries its current median. This batch wrapper performs those operations in order and returns every observed median, preserving streaming behavior without inventing unrelated scalar outputs.')]
SPECS.sort(key=lambda spec:tuple(map(int,spec['ref'].split('.'))))

SPECS += [dict(ref='17.4',method='MissingNumberBits',params=[('numbers','bits')],result='i',mutation='none',
 description='The hidden array contains every integer from 0 through n except one, in arbitrary order, where n=numbers.Size(). Find the missing integer using only individual-bit queries. The judge provides BitArray with Size() and FetchBit(index,column). Column 0 is the least significant bit; column 31 is the most significant bit of the nonnegative 32-bit value. Each FetchBit returns exactly 0 or 1.',
 constraints=['The hidden array has n in 0..10000 entries, all distinct, and is exactly {0,..,n} with one member omitted. You may read Size() and call FetchBit at index 0..n-1 and column 0..31. Empty input therefore has missing value 0.', 'The numeric array shown in examples/test fixtures is only a wire representation. Your callable receives BitArray, never an integer array. No full-integer accessor, mutable element, enumeration or private-state inspection is permitted. Use at most 4*n+32 FetchBit calls. Arithmetic on array indices, counts and the constructed missing answer is allowed; reconstructing every hidden integer is outside the bit-only linear-query task.', 'The provided hidden values and order are readonly. Local lists of original indices may be allocated and partitioned. Do not redeclare or replace the bank-provided BitArray API.'],
 why='At the least significant bit, a complete consecutive range has as many zeros as ones or one extra zero. Removing a zero leaves zero-count no larger than one-count; removing a one leaves more zeros. This identifies the missing low bit without reconstructing any number. Retain only indices with that bit, discard the known low bit conceptually, and repeat. Each retained set shrinks by roughly half.',
 correctness='At each stage the candidate values share the already determined low-bit prefix. Shifting away that prefix gives a consecutive range starting at zero with exactly the shifted missing value absent. Its even positions are either equal in count to its odd positions or one greater before removal. Therefore observed zeroCount<=oneCount identifies a missing zero bit, and the opposite inequality identifies a missing one bit. Keeping the selected partition preserves the consecutive-range-with-one-missing invariant for the next higher bit. When no candidates remain, the shifted complete range is {0}, so all remaining missing bits are zero. Accumulating the selected bits returns exactly the omitted integer. The geometric partition sizes give linear total bit queries, within the stated budget.',
 walkthrough='For hidden [0,1,3,4], n=4 and 2 is missing. At column 0, indices for values 0 and 4 have zero bits, while 1 and 3 have one bits. A tie selects missing bit 0 and keeps [0,4]. At column 1 both retained values have zero, so zeroCount>oneCount selects bit 1; the selected one partition is empty. Higher bits are zero, yielding binary 10 = 2.',example=([[0,1,3,4]],2),time='O(n) individual-bit queries and index-partition work across geometrically shrinking candidate sets; at most 32 bit positions in the fixed-width model.',space='O(n) auxiliary original-index lists; hidden integers are never reconstructed or copied into local numeric values.',edges=['Empty input, missing zero and missing n.','Shuffled values, boundaries around powers of two and n=10000.','Single-bit API index/column limits and the linear query budget.'],related='The public exercise permits querying one bit of a hidden integer at a time. A BitArray wrapper retains that restriction while specifying a conventional least-significant-first column numbering explicitly, instead of exposing the fixture integers as ordinary callable arguments.')]
SPECS.sort(key=lambda spec:tuple(map(int,spec['ref'].split('.'))))

SPECS += [dict(ref='17.12',method='ConvertBiNode',params=[('root','binode')],result='binode',mutation='bi-links',
 description='Convert a binary search tree of provided BiNode objects IN PLACE into a noncircular, sorted doubly linked list, and return its original first node. Before conversion Node1 is the left child and Node2 the right child. After conversion Node1 must be the previous node and Node2 the next node. Preserve every original node object and its Data value; allocate no replacement BiNode objects.',
 constraints=['The input is null or the root of one proper binary search tree with 0..1000 nodes. Every node has signed 32-bit Data and no cycles or shared children. Its inorder Data sequence is nondecreasing, so duplicates are allowed; the final list must preserve the original inorder OBJECT order even among equal values.', 'Return null for an empty tree. Otherwise return the original inorder-first node. Its Node1 must be null; the inorder-last Node2 must be null. Each other previous/next field must identify its actual adjacent original node, and both traversal directions must be consistent. Every input node appears exactly once.', 'An example/test wire fixture is {nodes:[[data,leftIndex,rightIndex],...],selected:rootIndex}, where -1 denotes null and row position identifies an original object. selected is -1 exactly for no nodes; every row belongs to the selected tree. The callable receives the actual BiNode root, not this fixture. Data is readonly; only Node1/Node2 links may change. C++ callers retain ownership: never delete provided nodes. Auxiliary stacks of original references are allowed.'],
 why='The BST inorder sequence is exactly the desired list order. An explicit traversal stack saves ancestors while descending left links. When visiting a node, save its original right child before changing links, connect it to the previously visited node, then traverse that saved right subtree. Saving the right child prevents the new list links from destroying the traversal route.',
 correctness='Before each visit, all nodes already visited form a correct doubly linked prefix in original inorder order, and the traversal stack plus saved current subtree contains exactly the unvisited continuation. Left descent reaches the next inorder node. Saving its original right link preserves the continuation; setting its previous link and the prior node next link extends the prefix with that exact original object. The first visited node becomes head with null previous. The last visited node is the original inorder maximum and has no remaining right subtree, so its next is explicitly null. Induction yields every original node once, unchanged Data and mutually consistent adjacent links, sorted by the BST inorder property. Null input performs no visits and returns null.',
 walkthrough='For original nodes A(Data=2) with Node1=B(Data=1) and Node2=C(Data=3), push A then B. Visit B first: head=B, B.Node1=null. Visit A next: B.Node2=A and A.Node1=B, while saved right=C remains available. Visit C: A.Node2=C, C.Node1=A and C.Node2=null. Return B, producing B↔A↔C with the same three objects.',example=([{'nodes':[[2,1,2],[1,-1,-1],[3,-1,-1]],'selected':0}],{'head':1,'order':[1,0,2],'values':[1,2,3],'previous':[-1,1,0],'next':[0,2,-1],'cycle':False}),time='O(n) time: each original node is pushed, popped and linked once.',space='O(h) auxiliary original-node references for tree height h; O(n) in a chain. The list reuses all original nodes and allocates no replacement nodes.',edges=['Null, one node, entirely left/right chains and a 1000-node tree.','Duplicate values with distinct object identities and full signed Data endpoints.','Original identity preservation, backward links, null endpoints and no cycles.'],related='The public BiNode task repurposes its two existing tree fields as doubly linked-list fields and returns the head. Identity and both link directions are observed here; a separately allocated sorted value list is not a valid substitute.')]
